Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how to use the `functions.B` helper to create a task queue with a concurrency of **5**.  
The queue is wired to emit two events – `saturated` and `unsaturated` – and we attach listeners that log the requested messages.  
Finally we enqueue a handful of simple tasks that just print a line to the console.

```js
// 1️⃣  Import the helper (the exact import path depends on your build setup)
//    For illustration we assume it is available as a global `functions` object.
const { B } = functions;

// 2️⃣  Create the queue with concurrency 5.
//     `e` (initial tasks) and `n` (payload size) are optional, so we omit them.
const queue = B({
  t: "5"          // concurrency level – note it is passed as a string
});

// 3️⃣  Attach event listeners for saturation changes.
queue.on('saturated', () => {
  console.log('Queue is saturated');
});

queue.on('unsaturated', () => {
  console.log('Queue is unsaturated');
});

// 4️⃣  Add some tasks.
//     Each task is a plain function that logs a message.
//     The queue will run up to 5 of them concurrently.
for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
  queue.add(() => {
    console.log(`Task ${i} is running`);
  });
}
```

### What’s happening under the hood?

| Step | What the code does | Why it matters |
|------|--------------------|----------------|
| `B({ t: "5" })` | Instantiates a new queue with a concurrency limit of 5. | The queue will only allow 5 tasks to run at the same time. |
| `queue.on('saturated', …)` | Registers a listener that fires when the queue’s active task count reaches the concurrency limit. | Lets you react to the queue being “full”. |
| `queue.on('unsaturated', …)` | Registers a listener that fires when the active task count drops below the limit. | Lets you react to the queue having room for more work. |
| `queue.add(fn)` | Adds a new task to the queue. | Tasks are executed automatically respecting the concurrency constraint. |

> **Tip** – If you need to pass an initial batch of tasks when creating the queue, you can supply them via the `e` property as a stringified array of functions (e.g. `e: "[() => console.log('Hello'), () => console.log('World')]"`). The helper will parse that string and enqueue the functions for you.

That’s all you need to get a fully‑functional, saturated/unsaturated‑aware queue up and running!

